+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Precision**: The backpropagation through the iterative ACID loop (up to $K_{ACID}$ iterations) can be computationally expensive and may suffer from vanishing or exploding gradients if not carefully implemented.
+*   **Boundaries**: The perturbation $e$ is constrained by the $L_2$ norm term in the cost function. The stopping criteria also include a comparison to the noise strength of single-network attacks, which serves as a practical bound for robustness evaluation [S1].
+*   **Degenerate Input**: If the ACID framework fails to converge within $K_{ACID}$ iterations, the gradient computation may be invalid.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Adversarial Robustness Evaluation
-*   **What it is:** The broader category of methods used to assess the stability of reconstruction networks against adversarial attacks.
-*   **Differentiation:** It focuses on computing BREN (Bias-Relative Error Norm) ratios for different perturbation strengths, providing a metric for robustness rather than just generating the attack.
-*   **When to choose:** When the goal is to quantify the robustness of a reconstruction method rather than to find the worst-case perturbation.
-*   **Link:** [Adversarial Robustness Evaluation](16421-adversarial-robustness-evaluation.md)
+The broader category of evaluating reconstruction networks against adversarial attacks by computing BREN ratios for different perturbation strengths. This method is a specific instance of that evaluation applied to the ACID framework. [Adversarial Robustness Evaluation](16421-adversarial-robustness-evaluation.md)
+
+### ACID Framework
+The Adaptive Compressive Iterative Deep framework that this attack targets. It combines deep reconstruction, compressed sensing, analytic mapping, and iterative refinement. [ACID Framework](link-to-acid-article)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method involves an outer loop of $K_{AA}$ iterations and an inner loop of $K_{ACID}$ iterations. Each inner iteration requires a full forward and backward pass through the ACID framework, which itself is iterative. Thus, the complexity is roughly $O(K_{AA} \cdot K_{ACID} \cdot C_{ACID})$, where $C_{ACID}$ is the cost of one ACID iteration.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the state of the ACID iterations for backpropagation, leading to memory usage proportional to $K_{ACID}$ times the size of the intermediate variables.
-*   **Strengths:** Evaluates the robustness of the entire ACID pipeline, including the stabilizing effects of compressed sensing and iterative refinement, which single-network attacks miss.
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive due to the nested iterative loops. The gradient computation is complex due to the branching structure of ACID.
-*   **When to use:** When assessing the security and stability of the ACID framework for tomographic reconstruction.
-*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are limited or when only the robustness of the deep learning component is of interest.
+*   **Time Complexity**: The method involves an outer loop of $K_{AA}$ iterations, each containing an inner loop of up to $K_{ACID}$ ACID iterations. The complexity is $O(K_{AA} \times K_{ACID})$ times the cost of a single ACID forward/backward pass.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Requires storing the state of the ACID framework for backpropagation, which may be significant if $K_{ACID}$ is large.
+*   **Strengths**: Provides a comprehensive assessment of the ACID framework's robustness by considering the entire reconstruction pipeline, including the interaction between data-driven priors and physical constraints.
+*   **Limitations**: Computationally expensive due to the nested iterative loops. The stopping criteria rely on a comparison to single-network attack strengths, which may not be directly comparable in all scenarios.
+*   **When to use**: When evaluating the robustness of the ACID framework against adversarial attacks in tomographic reconstruction.
+*   **When not to use**: When computational resources are limited or when only the robustness of a single component (e.g., the deep reconstruction network) is of interest.
 
 ## Background
-
-The ACID (Adaptive Compressive Iterative Deepening) framework was proposed to stabilize deep tomographic image reconstruction by combining deep learning with compressed sensing and analytic mapping [S1]. The adversarial attack method described here was introduced in the paper "Stabilizing deep tomographic reconstruction: Part B. Convergence analysis and adversarial attacks" to evaluate the robustness of this framework against adversarial perturbations [S1]. The work highlights that while ACID stabilizes the reconstruction, it is still susceptible to adversarial attacks, albeit potentially requiring different perturbation strategies than single neural networks.
+The ACID framework was proposed to stabilize deep tomographic image reconstruction by combining deep learning with compressed sensing and analytic mapping [S1]. The adversarial attack method described here was introduced to evaluate the robustness of this framework. The work is part of a series on stabilizing deep tomographic reconstruction, with this specific method detailed in "Stabilizing deep tomographic reconstruction: Part B. Convergence analysis and adversarial attacks" [S1]. The method uses gradient ascent to find perturbations that maximize the reconstruction error, leveraging the backpropagation algorithm through the ACID modules.
 
